  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nMainly clear skies are in place this morning across the region.\nA few areas of fog have developed, but surface obs indicate they\nare few and far between and aren't persistent. Temperatures will\nrebound back to near normal today with highs in the low to mid\n70s.\n\nA low amplitude trough will swing through tonight. Scattered\nshowers are expected to develop early this evening across \nwestern MN and increase in coverage as they track east tonight. \nAmounts won't be too impressive with some areas receiving a \ntenth to quarter of an inch. Another warm day Thursday with\nclearing skies and highs in the mid to upper 70s.\n\nThe disturbance responsible for the heavy rain threat Friday is\ncurrently located in the Gulf of California. NHC is monitoring\npotential development of it before it moves inland today. Given\nits origin, it will bring a lot of moisture with it as it \ntracks north across the Four Corners tonight, the western High \nPlains Thursday night, and MN Friday. Showers and thunderstorms\nwill develop over the Black Hills Thursday evening as the\ndisturbance reaches the High Plains. As it continues toward MN,\na 45 kt LLJ will develop ahead of it and transport +15C to +17C\ndewpoints at 850 mb northward. Pwats will increase to around 2 \ninches. The 700 mb low appears will track across central MN, \nwith the 850 mb low a bit farther south from northeastern SD to \nwest central WI, possibly over the metro. The track of the 850 \nmb low will determine where the 850 mb warm front sets up, and \nthis will influence where the heaviest rain will fall. While \nconfidence remains moderate for now, it appears models are \nstarting to hone in on the area most at risk. The lone outliers \nare the 00Z ECMWF AIFS and RRFS, which paint the axis of \nheaviest rain along the MN/IA border. The AIFS has been shifting\nnorth slightly in recent runs, however. The EPS-AIFS is farther\nnorth with its axis roughly from Redwood Falls to Red Wing. The\nAIGFS has been making remarkable shifts north each run of the \nlast five. The metro and north was dry 24 hours ago; now it is \nwithin the axis of heaviest amounts. The rest of the guidance \nsuite is in that same ballpark generally, with some smaller \ndisplacements north or south. \n\nThe 12Z ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index (EFI) from yesterday \nhighlights central/eastern SD through the southern third of \nMN for highly anomalous QPF for this time of year. The EFI is \n0.8-0.9, meaning a huge portion of ensemble members are shifted\ntoward the extreme end of the 5-week ECMWF reforecast \nclimatology over the past 20 years. Even more impressive, the \nShift of Tails (SOT) is up to 2 across portions of SD, which \nsignifies at least 10% of ensemble members are 2 standard \ndeviations above the 99th percentile of climatology. Simply put,\nthis may be an extreme event. The EFI and SOT charts are \navailable in 24-hour periods (00Z-00Z). Since our region will \nexperience heavy rain split between two forecast periods, the \nprobabilities naturally drop off as it passes through MN and \nwestern WI, but the threat is just as great here.\n\nConfidence is high most of the area will receive an inch of\nrain. Confidence begins to drop off with the placement of the\nheaviest axis and the magnitude of totals. For now it appears\nthe heaviest axis will likely fall from between Willmar and\nRedwood Falls, east to the southern TC metro, to the Eau Claire\nand La Crosse areas but this is subject to further shifts. As\nfor QPF, there is widespread agreement among many models that a\nstripe of at least 3 inches will fall somewhere and possibly \ngreater than 5 or 6. Despite the ongoing drought, this may \nresult in flooding concerns, particularly if it falls in an \nurban area. Flood watches may eventually be needed.\n\nThe front will temporarily settle south behind the Friday \nsystem, bringing drier weather Saturday. Another system, a \nlarger upper low over the Rockies, will push east Sunday into \nearly next week. This should lift the front back north and bring\nan unsettled period next week.",
